# Noseq architecture review

Reviewed: notes/2026-09-06-noseq-architecture.md at exact commit 2554c582db730cd3d64490970a573eab1463672e, artifact 0f3234d7, against request 87821661 (#46) and my promise 2bea7548 (#49). Reviewer: noseq-reviewer. This is a design review of a published draft. It changes no source and adopts nothing. Line numbers are those of the note at that commit.

## Verdict on readiness

The draft is a sound statement of intent and of the separation of concerns it inherits from gitseq and atseq: one order per instance, an opaque sequencer that never runs a business fold, retained definitions pinned by content, deterministic bounded interpretation, projections that are disposable caches. Those parts are ready to plan against.

It is not yet ready for a detailed implementation plan, because five decisions that the plan would have to build on are left open in the note itself, and each of them changes the wire format, the trust model or the acceptance gates. The note names most of them honestly as open (lines 519 to 523). A plan that starts before they are settled will either encode a guess or defer the hard part to the end of the spike. The gates in section G below are what the plan must close, and findings N1 to N5 are the ones that block it.

Ready to plan now: N6 onward can be bounded inside a spike with an explicit deferral or a fixed first-profile choice.

## Findings

Each finding gives the source lines, the evidence, a concrete failure scenario, a recommended decision, and whether it blocks implementation planning (blocks) or can be bounded inside the spike (bounded).

### N1. There is no threat model, so "confidential" has no testable meaning. Blocks.

Source: section 2 lines 84 to 89, section 6 lines 190 to 231, section 13 line 519.

Evidence: section 6 lists three service profiles and says the first opaque sequencer "may know submitter public keys, instance identifiers, payload sizes and ordering metadata" (lines 229 to 231), and that hiding submitter identity "is a separate profile" (line 231). Section 2 says the provider "can censor, delay or create conflicting signed histories" (line 86). Nowhere does the note say which adversaries the first prototype defends against, what each may observe, and what the acceptance tests will prove they cannot learn. "Confidentiality profile" is listed as a thing to settle before implementation (line 519) but is not settled.

Failure scenario: the spike ships the encrypted-payloads profile. The provider (or anyone who reads the primary relay, since read access rules are also undecided, lines 319 to 322) can enumerate every submitter key, every submission size and time, and the exact membership of every instance. A user who was promised "confidential collaboration" (line 42) learns after the fact that participation itself was public. Gate 7 (line 512) passes because content stayed encrypted, and the design is judged a success against a property nobody defined.

Recommended decision: write a one-page threat model as the first plan deliverable, with a table of adversaries (provider, primary relay reader, mirror operator, removed member, compromised device, network observer) against assets (content, submitter identity, membership, instance existence, timing and sizes, definitions) stating hidden, visible or bounded for the first profile. Fix the first profile as "encrypted payloads over a private relay with authenticated reads", state plainly that membership and submitter identity are visible to the provider, and make gate 7 assert exactly that. Add the stronger profile as later work.

### N2. Forward secrecy and replayable history are in direct conflict and the note defers the reconciliation. Blocks.

Source: section 6 lines 215 to 221, section 4 lines 155 to 160, section 13 line 510 to 513.

Evidence: the note states the conflict correctly: "Once old decryption material is erased, a fresh device cannot simply download ciphertext and replay everything" (lines 216 to 217) and requires "a history-sharing, encrypted-archive or snapshot policy" (lines 217 to 218). It also requires that "projections can be discarded and rebuilt when the retained history and its required recovery material remain available" (lines 158 to 160) and that a snapshot "is not equivalent to independently replaying all original actions" (lines 220 to 221). It does not choose. MLS (RFC 9420) deletes old epoch secrets by design; a member added at epoch n has no key material for epochs before n, and the protocol has no history-sharing primitive. So under an MLS group, gate 6 (rebuild from retained history) and gate 7 (new-member admission with a declared history policy) cannot both be satisfied by the same mechanism unless the application layer defines one. The primary sources say so in as many words: RFC 9750 section 6.7 states that MLS "does not provide direct support for restoring history" and that "applications can elect to provide such a mechanism outside of MLS", and the adopted Marmot specification's retained-history page says complete long-term history "need[s] a separate protocol mechanism" and that application payloads outside its past-epoch window "MUST expire". That last point widens the finding: the archive is needed for every participant who is offline past the window, not only for late joiners.

Failure scenario: the group uses MLS with default key deletion. A member's only device is lost. They recover their signing key from backup (section 10 line 421 says this "may not restore historical decryption") and rejoin at epoch k. Every action before epoch k is ciphertext they cannot open; the fold pauses at position 1 forever, exactly the "missing decryption material pauses progress" rule (line 157). The application is unrecoverable for that user, and a second member removal makes it unrecoverable for everyone who joins later.

Recommended decision: choose one of three explicit policies for the first prototype and write it into the definition's confidentiality policy (section 9 line 345): (a) no forward secrecy for history, meaning a long-lived per-instance content key wrapped to each admitted member, so any admitted device replays everything, and removal only stops future reading; (b) MLS for live traffic plus an encrypted archive keyed separately, re-encrypted and re-shared on admission, where the archive is the replay substrate and MLS is transport; or (c) MLS with snapshots, accepting that late joiners trust a signed snapshot rather than replaying. Option (a) is the smallest and keeps gate 6 honest; it should be the spike's choice with (b) as the documented upgrade. Whatever is chosen, gate 6 must state which recovery material a rebuild needs and gate 7 must state what a late joiner can and cannot decrypt.

### N3. The signed wire format, proof retention and retry identity are all "to be specified" while every other section depends on them. Blocks.

Source: section 7 lines 241 to 270, section 5 lines 178 to 181, section 9 lines 348 to 349.

Evidence: the entry is given "conceptually" (lines 241 to 249) with no bytes, no hash, no signature scheme, and no statement of what is a Nostr event and what is inside one. Lines 251 to 256 make the right observation that a NIP-59 inner rumor is unsigned, and that actor proof therefore has to be specified at the application layer, but do not specify it. Line 261 says the stable submission ID is looked up for retries, and line 267 says "randomized encryption must not turn a transport retry into another logical action", but nothing says what the ID is derived from. If it is derived from ciphertext, every re-encryption is a new action; if from plaintext, it leaks a plaintext hash to the sequencer; if a client-chosen random nonce carried in the outer envelope, that must be said, together with who checks that the same nonce is never reused with different content and how the sequencer detects it without plaintext (it can compare ciphertext bytes only if the client retries the identical envelope, which line 267 requires).

Failure scenario: the client encrypts the same action twice because the first submission's reply was lost (a required case, gate 3 line 505). The second envelope has different ciphertext. The sequencer has no plaintext-independent identity to match against, sequences it again, and the fold applies the action twice. The exact-retry property that atseq proved with a plaintext nonce (its retry index is keyed on app, actor key and nonce) does not carry over automatically once the nonce is inside the encrypted payload.

Recommended decision: specify, before planning, one envelope with these fields in canonical bytes: instance genesis identity, submitter public key, client-chosen submission nonce in the clear, ciphertext, and a submitter signature over all of the above. The sequencer keys its retry index on (genesis, submitter, nonce) and returns the original receipt for an identical envelope, refusing a different envelope under the same nonce. Actor proof for the fold is then the outer signature, retained with the entry; the inner plaintext carries no second signature and the NIP-59 rumor problem does not arise because no rumor is used. Decide whether the entry is a Nostr event signed by the sequencer key (kind, tags, content encoding, id computation per NIP-01) or a separate structure carried in a Nostr event's content, and write the canonical-bytes test vectors as the first spike deliverable, as atseq S1 did. Three facts from the sources constrain that choice. A Nostr signature covers only the six canonical fields, so sequence number and predecessor identity must sit in tags or content to be signed. The journal kind must be a regular kind (1000 to 9999) or the mirror may discard entries; addressable kinds such as 30078 keep only the latest version. Common relay defaults cap a normalised event at 64 KiB (strfry) or 128 KiB (nostr-rs-relay), and NIP-44 pads plaintext to a power of two before base64, so an encrypted action of about 24 KiB is the practical ceiling per entry; definitions and assets need blob storage from the start, as section 8 lines 330 to 333 already allow.

### N4. Membership authority is not located. Blocks.

Source: section 6 lines 223 to 227, section 9 lines 345 and 363 to 365, section 10 lines 408 to 412, section 13 line 512.

Evidence: the note separates relay admission (who may submit and read at the sequencer) from encrypted-group membership (who can decrypt) and says "relay admission alone cannot grant decryption" (lines 409 to 410). It says "application authority grants membership" (line 408) and the genesis carries "initial authority" (line 345). It does not say whether membership changes are (a) actions in the application log, sequenced and folded like any other, (b) MLS commits outside the log, or (c) both, and if both, which is authoritative when they disagree. Under (b), the fold cannot know who was a member at position p, so per-action permission checks (line 239 "application permission") have no input. Under (a), the group encryption layer must be driven by the fold, which means the client cannot decrypt an action until it has folded the membership history up to that point, and a removed member's client would still receive ciphertext it is not supposed to have.

Failure scenario: an admin removes Bob. The MLS commit excludes Bob from the next epoch. The log contains no record of the removal. Alice's fold, replaying later, still believes Bob is a member at positions after the removal and accepts an action Bob managed to submit through the sequencer (which only checks outer admission, line 237) before the sequencer's allowlist was updated. Different clients that updated their allowlists at different times fold different outcomes for the same log. Gate 2 fails in a way that is hard to reproduce.

Recommended decision: make membership an application-level control action in the log, interpreted under the preceding authority, exactly as atseq's activation is, and make the group encryption epoch change a consequence recorded with it (the MLS commit's reference, or the wrapped content key for policy (a) in N2). The sequencer's admission allowlist is derived from the folded membership by an authorized client and is advisory transport policy, not authority. Say so in section 2, and add a gate: "a member removed at position p has any later submission recorded as sequenced but ineffective on every client".

### N5. The Durable Object relay is a second protocol implementation with no bounded surface. Blocks the "smallest sequencer" claim; bounded if the surface is fixed.

Source: section 8 lines 292 to 298, 300 to 306, 317 to 322.

Evidence: the note proposes that the per-instance object "also serves the primary Nostr relay: history reads, authenticated subscriptions and live delivery" (lines 292 to 293) and admits this "is work for the prototype, not an existing DO feature" (line 294). It does not fix which NIP-01 messages, filters, kinds and limits the object must honour, nor whether the mirror (strfry) must accept sequencer-signed events with whatever kind and tag structure N3 chooses. It asks the prototype to "respect NIP-01 endpoint semantics instead of inventing invisible path-dependent relay behavior" (lines 297 to 298), which is exactly the tension: one relay endpoint per instance means either one hostname or path per instance, and a generic Nostr client expects one relay to serve many kinds and authors.

Failure scenario: the spike implements REQ with the filters its own client uses. The mirror rejects the sequencer-signed events because their kind is in a range strfry's default policy drops, or because the events carry a tag structure the mirror indexes differently; the "independent retrieval from the configured mirror" gate (line 509) is then tested only against a mirror configured by the same author, and the design's portability claim rests on that configuration.

Recommended decision: fix the relay surface as a written subset before planning: which client messages (REQ, EVENT, CLOSE, AUTH), which filters (ids, authors, kinds, since, until, limit, and one tag filter for the genesis), the event kind or kinds noseq uses, maximum event size, and the exact NIP-11 information document the object serves. Write conformance tests for that subset that run against both the object and strfry with a stock configuration, and put "sequencer-signed events accepted by an unmodified strfry with the documented policy file" into gate 5. Two source facts simplify this. NIP-01 forbids extra path segments only when they do not change behaviour, so a Worker routing one path per instance to its object is conformant and distinct hosts (lines 296 to 298) are not required. NIP-70's protected-event tag makes a stock relay refuse the entry unless its author authenticates, which gives the mirror a standard "only the sequencer writes this instance" rule without custom relay code; the note does not mention it. One fact complicates it: strfry restricts reads by kind only for kinds listed in its configuration, and its read-policy semantics are not documented, so a stock mirror cannot enforce "only members of instance X read instance X" and the confidential-mirror question in lines 319 to 322 is real.

### N6. Deterministic execution is asserted but the numeric and value profile for geometry is left open. Bounded.

Source: section 3 lines 115 to 119, section 4 lines 143 to 153, section 13 lines 484 to 486 and 516 to 517.

Evidence: the note correctly says atseq's profile admits only safe integers and plain JSON (lines 144 to 145), that a fold emitting typed buffers needs a new profile covering rounding, byte order, layout, non-finite values and limits (lines 150 to 153), and that the first geometry demonstration can convert fixed-point integers to Float32Array in the presentation adapter (lines 149 to 150). It then chooses "an encrypted collaborative spatial board" whose folds "produce directly renderable JSON and a geometry-buffer presentation" as the first application (lines 484 to 486) without saying which of the two paths it takes.

Failure scenario: two clients on different engines fold a rotation with a JSONata float expression; their Float32Array outputs differ in the last bit; gate 2 compares projections and fails intermittently, and the team spends the spike on float determinism instead of confidentiality.

Recommended decision: for the spike, keep the atseq profile unchanged (integers only), represent coordinates as fixed-point integers in the fold, and convert to Float32Array only in the adapter, which is outside the compared projection. Compare projections as JSON before conversion. Declare the typed-buffer profile as deferred with its own future gate. This is the note's own suggestion at line 149; it should be the decision, not a maybe.

### N7. "Direct UI projection" needs the same capability boundary the note gives definitions, and the note does not give it. Bounded.

Source: section 4 lines 128 to 141, section 3 lines 117 to 119, section 12 line 464 and 470.

Evidence: projections are to be consumed by "direct D3 data binding or trusted SVG rendering" and "three.js or another graphics adapter" (lines 132 to 133). Section 3 says "a rendering primitive cannot expose signing keys or arbitrary network access" (lines 117 to 118). Atseq achieved that by rendering only a fixed allow-list of primitives with text nodes; a D3 or three.js adapter that binds arbitrary fold output is a general renderer. The note does not say which adapter code is trusted host code, which is application content, and what an adapter may do with a value that is a string.

Failure scenario: a definition's fold emits a label containing markup or a URL; the D3 adapter binds it into an SVG text node with innerHTML or sets an href; the "trusted SVG rendering" becomes a script or exfiltration path from a confidential application, defeating N1's whole purpose.

Recommended decision: adapters are host code, versioned with the runtime profile, and each adapter's accepted projection schema is declared and validated before binding, as atseq validates query results against a Lexicon. Strings are always text nodes; no attribute that resolves a URL or executes is ever bound from projection data. Put a hostile-projection test in gate 9 the way atseq's S4 has a hostile-view test.

### N8. Application extensibility: definition activation is deferred without a stale-submission rule. Bounded.

Source: section 3 lines 121 to 124.

Evidence: the note says the prototype "may initially keep the definition fixed" but "must declare how stale-definition submissions behave before adding updates" (lines 123 to 124). That is right, and it is cheap to declare now: atseq's rule (an intent naming a definition other than the active one is sequenced and recorded ineffective as definition_changed) transfers directly and its regression tests exist.

Recommended decision: adopt the atseq rule verbatim for the first prototype, name the definition CID in every submission's clear envelope (N3) so the sequencer can retain it and the fold can judge it, and defer activation to the second spike with atseq's S5 as the reference design including its closure-transport lesson.

### N9. Invitation and device onboarding conflate three things the note itself separates. Bounded.

Source: section 10 lines 385 to 431, section 11 lines 441 to 458.

Evidence: the note wants "open an invitation, choose a name, approve Join, and see the application" (lines 385 to 386) and says immediate unattended admission "needs a reviewed invitation/key-delivery design; it is not supplied by the opaque sequencer" (lines 411 to 412), and that "the first implementation must state whether an existing device must be online" (lines 410 to 411). Under policy (a) in N2 an invitation can carry the wrapped content key only if the inviter's device wraps it to the invitee's key, which requires knowing the invitee's key before they exist, or a two-step join (request, then approval by an online member). The note's Buzz table (lines 443 to 449) shows Buzz solved this with a server-side invitation service that admits a public key at redemption, which is relay admission, not decryption.

Failure scenario: the invitee opens the link on a fresh browser, creates a key, and sees "waiting for a member to admit you" with no member online for a day. The desired experience fails, and the fallback (an invitation that embeds decryption material in the link) makes the link itself the secret, which the note's confidentiality profile never analysed.

The sources settle the "must a device be online" question for the MLS path: NIP-EE tracks each device as a separate member, Marmot's multi-device design is a branch draft that "MUST NOT be implemented for interop yet", and an Add needs an existing member to commit and send the Welcome. Immediate unattended admission is therefore not available under Marmot at all.

Recommended decision: decide the two-step join for the first prototype: the invitation link carries genesis, routing and a one-time invitation token; redemption submits the invitee's public key to the sequencer's admission list; an online authorized member's client (or the creator's) observes the pending join and issues the membership control action (N4) that wraps the key material. State that an existing device must be online, and make gate 8 test exactly that flow plus its "no one online" state. Treat the link-embeds-secret variant as the later profile.

### N10. "Opaque sequencer" plus equivocation detection is claimed without a mechanism. Bounded.

Source: section 2 lines 86 to 88, section 7 lines 277 to 281.

Evidence: "Comparing conflicting histories can reveal equivocation; signatures cannot prevent it" (lines 86 to 87). "Signed tips can be discovered" (line 278). Nothing says who compares, when, or what a client does on detection. Atseq's folder refuses rollback and fork of a verified prefix and pauses; that is the minimum.

Recommended decision: state the rule: a client retains the longest verified prefix it has seen, refuses any entry whose predecessor differs from the retained one at the same position, and surfaces a fork as a verification problem with no automatic trust reset. Gossip of tips through the mirror gives cross-client detection for free once both relays are read. Put it into gate 3's "conflicting histories" case, which already exists.

### N11. Durable Object lifecycle assumptions need three corrections. Bounded.

Source: section 8 lines 285 to 306.

Evidence: the note relies on "SQLite-backed durable storage", "server-side WebSocket hibernation", "alarms run at least once and have bounded automatic retries" and "outbound WebSockets cannot hibernate". These are broadly right, subject to the fact sheet in section F. Three consequences are not drawn: (1) the object's in-memory state is lost on eviction, so the sequencer's serialized-append boundary (line 268 to 269) must be re-established from storage on every wake, which is only safe if the tip is read inside the same storage transaction that writes the next entry; (2) a hibernated subscription connection wakes the object on every inbound message, so live delivery to n subscribers costs one wake per published event per object, which is fine, but a client that polls for freshness (line 280) can keep the object hot; (3) the per-object storage and request limits bound the size of one instance's history and the burst rate of one instance, which is exactly what the note's "one order per instance" needs, but the plan has to measure them and state the limits the way atseq S6 did.

The Durable Objects documentation supports the note's assumptions and adds facts the plan needs: storage methods are transactional and the output gate holds outgoing messages until writes are flushed, which gives "durable before reply" for free; an await in the append path is an interleaving point unless guarded, so signing must sit inside the storage transaction or a blockConcurrencyWhile; at most six outgoing connections, with an open outbound socket billed for up to 15 minutes; a 16 KiB serialised attachment per hibernated socket, which bounds retained subscription filters; a soft limit of 1,000 requests per second per object, with inbound WebSocket messages billed at a 20 to 1 ratio; placement fixed at creation with best-effort hints, so provisioning should pass the creator's location hint; and on the paid plan rows written are the dominant cost, so the four rows per entry (entry, retry index, tip, outbox) are the number to watch. The free plan runs a prototype.

Recommended decision: the plan's smallest deployment measures append latency, wake latency after idle, and cost per 1,000 entries on one object, and the sequencer spike writes tip and entry in one transaction and proves resumption after forced eviction (gate 4 already covers crash after commit; add eviction between commit and publish).

### N12. Tests and the smallest useful deployment are described only as gates. Bounded.

Source: section 13 lines 500 to 517.

Evidence: the nine gates are good and map to atseq's, but none names a command, an evidence file, or a bound, and gates 7 and 8 have no reference implementation to compare against. The atseq series showed that a gate without a machine-readable retained result and a source-hash pin gets refreshed by later runs and loses its provenance; the plan should adopt atseq's evidence helper shape from the start.

Recommended decision: each gate gets a command under package.json, a retained JSON result with source hashes, and a stated bound or "observation only". The smallest deployment is: one Worker plus one Durable Object class deployed to a single Cloudflare account, one strfry mirror in a container, the generic browser host served by the Worker, and the CLI adapter; nothing else. Provision one instance by hand for the spike; the provisioning API of section 9 is a second spike.

### N13. Ecosystem reuse claims are stated with the right caution but two are decisions in disguise. Bounded.

Source: section 12 lines 462 to 480, section 13 line 495.

Evidence: "Start with Applesauce as client plumbing" (line 495) and "investigate Marmot" (line 495) are the only two concrete library choices, and both are made before the wire format (N3) or the group policy (N2). Applesauce's models are reactive observables over Nostr events; noseq's client needs verified, ordered, decrypted entries, which is a different input. Marmot is MLS-over-Nostr for messaging; its data model (welcome messages, key packages, group events) may or may not carry an application log. Section F's fact sheet reports what their current documentation actually states.

The sources change the Marmot picture more than the note allows. NIP-EE is marked unrecommended and superseded by the Marmot repository, whose adopted specification (August 2026) moved key packages to an addressable kind, replaced the NIP-44 outer layer with a ChaCha20-Poly1305 layer keyed from the MLS exporter, allows only an h tag on group messages, and replaced the account-identity proof; marmot-ts is alpha, last moved in June 2026, and predates that restructure; MDK is current but Rust. NIP-07 and NIP-46 signers cannot produce the raw BIP-340 signatures Marmot's identity proof needs, so a browser host must hold its own key for MLS regardless of how the Nostr identity is signed. Marmot's own rules say relay order "MUST NOT choose group state", so a sequencer that totally orders MLS commits is a stronger delivery service than Marmot assumes; either the sequencer carries commits as opaque payloads and Marmot converges on its own, or the two orderings are reconciled explicitly. Wrapping MLS bytes in a sequencer-signed event is a new Marmot transport binding, not a use of the Nostr one.

Recommended decision: make both provisional and time-boxed in the plan: a two-day integration check for each, with a stated fallback (plain nostr-tools relay plumbing; policy (a) content-key wrapping without MLS). The note already says libraries were "not installed or interoperability-tested" (line 480); the plan must not inherit them as settled.

## Gates the plan must close

G1 (N1): a written threat model with the first profile's visible and hidden facts, and gate 7 rewritten to assert them.
G2 (N2): a chosen history-recovery policy, the recovery material a rebuild needs, and what a late joiner can decrypt.
G3 (N3): canonical envelope and entry bytes with test vectors, the retry identity in the clear, and the actor proof retained with the entry.
G4 (N4): membership as a logged control action with the sequencer allowlist derived from it, and a removed-member test.
G5 (N5): a fixed relay subset with conformance tests against the object and an unmodified strfry.
G6 (N6, N7): integer-only profile with adapter-side conversion, validated projection schemas, and a hostile-projection test.
G7 (N8): the stale-definition rule adopted and tested; activation deferred.
G8 (N9): the two-step join with an online-member requirement stated and tested, including the no-member-online state.
G9 (N10, N11): fork refusal on a verified prefix; single-transaction tip and entry write; resumption after eviction between commit and publish; measured append, wake and cost figures.
G10 (N12, N13): every gate has a command and a retained hashed result; library choices are time-boxed with fallbacks.

## What is sound and should be kept

The separation of order, interpretation, publication and activation (section 2 and 7); the pinned dependency closure and retained old definitions (section 3 lines 104 to 108); the atomic advance of projection, outcomes and frontier and the pause rule for missing history or keys (section 4 lines 155 to 160); the honest statements about NIP-59's unsigned rumor (lines 253 to 256), EOSE and addressable events (lines 169 to 172), removal not withdrawing learned data (lines 223 to 224), snapshots not being replay (lines 219 to 221), and provisioning IDs being implementation details (lines 367 to 369). The nine gates are the right list; they need commands and bounds.

## Ecosystem and platform claims checked against primary sources

The attached fact sheet (noseq-fact-sheet.md) checks every ecosystem and platform claim in the note against the current primary source, with the source's own date, a verdict of supported, partly, unsupported, outdated or silent, and the consequence for the design. In summary: every claim the note makes about NIP-01, NIP-42, NIP-44, NIP-59, NIP-78, NIP-89, RFC 9420, Durable Objects, strfry, Nosflare, Blossom, Buzz and the client libraries is supported, most of them verbatim. Two references are outdated: NIP-EE as the description of MLS over Nostr, and marmot-ts as an interoperable client. One claim is stricter than the source: NIP-01 permits path-dependent relay endpoints. Two standard mechanisms are absent from the note and would help: NIP-70 protected events for sequencer-only writes at the mirror, and NIP-11 limitation fields for advertising size limits (though NIP-11 has no retention field, so retention stays an out-of-band contract). Seven points could not be confirmed from primary sources and are listed in the sheet's last table.
